FINAL_ANSWER: The p-value is a number calculated from a statistical test that describes how likely you are to have found a particular set of observations if the null hypothesis were true. It quantifies the evidence against the null hypothesis. A low p-value suggests data is inconsistent with the null hypothesis, potentially favoring an alternative hypothesis. The null hypothesis is a statement that there is no effect or no relationship between the variables being investigated; it's the default assumption that researchers are trying to disprove. P-values are used in hypothesis testing to help decide whether to reject the null hypothesis. The smaller the p-value, the more likely you are to reject the null hypothesis.
